\title{Allocation, Deferral, and the Exact Arithmetic\\ of an Adaptive Quadratic Sieve}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{31 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete constants-layer theory of adaptive scheduling for a sieve in which a
cheap dial predicts each target's relation rate. The motivating measurement is an adaptive
quadratic sieve whose quadratic-residue dial is well calibrated (rank correlation $0.739$
against realised per-target yield, oracle dial $0.778$, larger factor base $0.835$), yet in
which reallocating sieve length in inverse proportion to the predicted rate \emph{lost}
$17.6\%$ of the yield, and $146.7\%$ with a floor clip removed, while a rate concentrator
gained $8.6\%$ and a realised oracle bound sat $74.8\%$ above baseline. We prove all four
signs are forced by the allocation model alone, with no reference to calibration error: the
inverse-rate policy yields the budget times the \emph{harmonic} mean of the rates and the
uniform baseline the budget times the \emph{arithmetic} mean; the clipped family is affine
in the floor with nonnegative slope $\sum r - n^2/\sum r^{-1}$; the concentrator attains the
exact oracle bound $B\,r_{\max}$. We then analyse the deployment that worked --- using the
dial to \emph{defer} --- proving \emph{retention $\ge$ work fraction} under separation, a
linear discordance budget for arbitrary dials, its refinement by the \emph{inversion mass},
and an exact identity expressing the retention deficit as inversion mass minus concordance
mass. We show the policy space collapses to a single threshold, with excess over a minimal
schedule exactly the tie class. Finally we compute the arithmetic exactly: for odd $p$ with
$N \not\equiv 0$, $x^2 \equiv N \pmod p$ has exactly $2$ or $0$ solutions, so the per-period
rate is exactly $2/p$ or $0$; hence the aggregate rate of an admissible base $A$ is $2H_A$,
the oracle target is $\min A$, and the headroom ratio is exactly $|A|/(p_{\min}H_A)$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} quadratic sieve, adaptive allocation, AM--HM inequality,
harmonic mean, rank concordance, inversion mass, quadratic residues, threshold policy.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

A sieve-based factoring run distributes a fixed computational budget over many independent
targets, each returning relations at an unknown rate. A natural idea is a cheap
\emph{dial}: a predictor of the per-target rate computable before expensive work begins.

In the run motivating this paper the dial counted quadratic residues over small primes and
performed well: Spearman rank correlation $0.739$ against realised per-target yield, versus
$0.778$ for an oracle dial on the same noisy realisation, and $0.835$ on a factor base of
$100$ primes. Twenty of twenty targets factored end to end, with $1350$ relations
independently reverified. Four allocation outcomes were measured against the uniform
baseline:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
policy & outcome \\
\midrule
sieve length $\propto 1/\text{predicted rate}$, floor clip on & $-17.6\%$ \\
same, floor clip removed & $-146.7\%$ \\
rate concentrator & $+8.6\%$ \\
realised oracle bound & $+74.8\%$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Separately, a \emph{deployment flip}: thresholding the dial at its twentieth percentile
skipped $28.3\%$ of the work while retaining $89.5\%$ of the relations, a $28.9\%$
throughput gain. A hard tail of $40$ of $400$ targets was unreachable at any sieve depth.

\subsection{The question, and the answer}

The naive reading is that rank correlation $0.74$ is too weak to steer by. That reading is
wrong in a strong sense: \emph{the losing policy loses for every rate vector, including one
predicted perfectly.} The sign of the inverse-rate rule is a property of the rule, and the
floor clip is a coordinate on an affine family whose slope is exactly the AM--HM gap.

All statements below are exact inequalities and identities about a finite allocation model:
no asymptotics, no probabilistic assumptions, and no calibration hypotheses beyond those
named.

\section{The allocation model}

Fix a finite set $s$ of targets, $|s| = n \ge 1$.

\begin{definition}[Rates, allocations, yield]
A \emph{rate vector} is $r : s \to \mathbb{R}$, where $r_i$ is relations produced per unit
sieve length on target $i$. An \emph{allocation} is $\ell : s \to \mathbb{R}$. The
\emph{yield} is $Y(r,\ell) = \sum_{i \in s} r_i \ell_i$. An allocation is
\emph{admissible for budget $B$} if $\ell \ge 0$ and $\sum_i \ell_i = B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Policies]
For a budget $B$:
\begin{itemize}
\item uniform baseline $\ell^{\mathrm{unif}}_i = B/n$;
\item inverse-rate policy (for $r>0$)
  $\ell^{\mathrm{inv}}_i = B\, r_i^{-1}\big/\sum_{j} r_j^{-1}$;
\item clipped inverse-rate policy with floor $f$,
  $\ell^{f}_i = f + (B - nf)\, r_i^{-1}\big/\sum_j r_j^{-1}$;
\item concentrator on $i_0$: $\ell^{\mathrm{conc}}_i = B$ if $i = i_0$, else $0$.
\end{itemize}
All are budget-normalised, and $\ell^0 = \ell^{\mathrm{inv}}$, $\ell^{B/n} = \ell^{\mathrm{unif}}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Throughput]
For nonempty $K$ sieved at a common length, $\Th(K) = \frac{1}{|K|}\sum_{i\in K} r_i$.
\end{definition}

\section{The inverse-rate policy must lose}

\begin{proposition}[Uniform yield]
$Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}}) = \frac{B}{n}\sum_{i} r_i = B\,\AM(r)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Inverse-rate yield]
If $r > 0$ on a nonempty $s$, then
$Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{inv}}) = \dfrac{Bn}{\sum_i r_i^{-1}} = B\,\HM(r)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Write $T = \sum_j r_j^{-1} > 0$. Each summand is $r_i \cdot (Br_i^{-1}/T) = B/T$,
independent of $i$; summing $n$ equal terms gives $Bn/T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The inverse-rate policy is precisely the \emph{equalising} policy: every target contributes
the same number of relations. That is what makes it attractive and what makes it lose.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[AM--HM, symmetrised]\label{thm:amhm}
Let $r_i > 0$ on $s$, $|s| = n$. Then
$n^2 \le \big(\sum_i r_i\big)\big(\sum_i r_i^{-1}\big)$, strictly if $r_a \ne r_b$ for some
$a,b \in s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expanding over ordered pairs,
$\big(\sum_i r_i\big)\big(\sum_j r_j^{-1}\big) = \sum_{(i,j)} r_i r_j^{-1}$, and equally
$= \sum_{(i,j)} r_j r_i^{-1}$. Adding,
\[
2\Big(\sum_i r_i\Big)\Big(\sum_j r_j^{-1}\Big) = \sum_{(i,j) \in s\times s}\Big(\frac{r_i}{r_j} + \frac{r_j}{r_i}\Big).
\]
For $x,y>0$, $\frac{x}{y}+\frac{y}{x}-2 = \frac{(x-y)^2}{xy} \ge 0$, strictly when
$x \ne y$. Hence each of the $n^2$ ordered pairs contributes at least $2$, giving
$2\big(\sum r\big)\big(\sum r^{-1}\big) \ge 2n^2$; and if $r_a \ne r_b$ the pair $(a,b)$
contributes strictly more.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inverse-rate reallocation never wins]
For $r > 0$ on a nonempty $s$ and $B \ge 0$,
$Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{inv}}) \le Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The claim is $Bn/T \le B\Sigma/n$ with $\Sigma = \sum r$, $T = \sum r^{-1} > 0$; cross
multiplying by $T, n > 0$ it becomes $Bn^2 \le B\Sigma T$, which is
Theorem~\ref{thm:amhm} times $B \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict loss under an informative dial]
If additionally $r_a \ne r_b$ for some $a,b \in s$ and $B > 0$, then
$Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{inv}}) < Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The rate vector here is the \emph{true} one; no predictor appears. A perfectly calibrated
dial deployed as inverse-rate reallocation still loses, strictly, exactly in the regime
where the dial carries information. The measured $-17.6\%$ is a measurement of
$1 - \HM(r)/\AM(r) = 1 - n^2/\big(\sum r\big)\big(\sum r^{-1}\big)$, a scale-invariant
functional of the rate distribution alone.
\end{remark}

\section{The floor clip is a coordinate, not a hack}

\begin{theorem}[Affine clipped yield]\label{thm:clip}
For nonempty $s$, positive rates, and any $B, f$, with $\Sigma = \sum_i r_i$ and
$T = \sum_i r_i^{-1}$,
\[
Y\big(r,\ell^{f}\big) \;=\; \frac{Bn}{T} \;+\; f\Big(\Sigma - \frac{n^2}{T}\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Termwise $r_i \ell^f_i = r_i f + (B - nf)/T$; summing gives
$f\Sigma + n(B-nf)/T = Bn/T + f(\Sigma - n^2/T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Endpoints]
$Y(\ell^{0}) = B\,\HM(r)$ and $Y(\ell^{B/n}) = B\,\AM(r)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity of the clip]
With positive rates on a nonempty $s$, $f \mapsto Y(r,\ell^{f})$ is nondecreasing, and
strictly increasing as soon as two rates differ.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:clip} the map is affine with slope $\Sigma - n^2/T$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:amhm} and $T > 0$ this is $\ge 0$, and $>0$ when two rates differ.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The measured $-17.6\%$ and $-146.7\%$ are two points of one straight line: removing the clip
travels from an interior point of the segment
$[\ell^{\mathrm{inv}}, \ell^{\mathrm{unif}}]$ to its lower endpoint. No clip value pushes
the family above the baseline, since the maximum over $f \in [0,B/n]$ is the baseline
itself.
\end{remark}

\section{The concentrator and the exact oracle bound}

\begin{proposition}
If $i_0 \in s$ then $Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{conc}}) = r_{i_0}B$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The correct sign]
If $r_i \le r_{i_0}$ for all $i \in s$ and $B \ge 0$, then
$Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}}) \le Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{conc}})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Oracle bound, attained]
If $r_i \le r_{i_0}$ on $s$, $\ell \ge 0$ and $\sum_i \ell_i = B$, then
$Y(r,\ell) \le r_{i_0}B$; the concentrator on $i_0$ attains it. Hence
$\sup\{Y(r,\ell)\} = B\,r_{\max}$ over admissible allocations, and the supremum is a
maximum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_i r_i \ell_i \le \sum_i r_{i_0}\ell_i = r_{i_0}B$ termwise, using $\ell_i \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact headroom]
For nonempty $s$,
$B\,r_{\max} - Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}}) = B\big(r_{\max} - \tfrac1n\sum_i r_i\big)$, and
for nonnegative rates $B\,r_{\max} \le n\,Y(r,\ell^{\mathrm{unif}})$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Deferral: the flip that wins}

\begin{definition}[Threshold sets]
For a dial $d : s \to \mathbb{R}$ and $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$,
$K_\theta = \{i \in s : d_i \ge \theta\}$ and $D_\theta = \{i \in s : d_i < \theta\}$; they
partition $s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Separation and concordance]
A split $s = K \sqcup D$ is \emph{separated with slack $c$} if $r_j \le r_i + c$ for all
$i \in K$, $j \in D$. A dial $d$ is \emph{concordant} with $r$ on $s$ if
$d_i < d_j \Rightarrow r_i \le r_j$. Any monotone transform of $r$ is concordant; in
particular $r$ is concordant with itself.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Separation engine]\label{thm:sep}
If $s = K \sqcup D$ and $r_j \le r_i + c$ for all $i\in K$, $j \in D$, then
\[
|K|\sum_{i\in s} r_i \;\le\; |s|\sum_{i \in K} r_i \;+\; c\,|K|\,|D|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\Sigma_K = \sum_K r$, $\Sigma_D = \sum_D r$. Summing the hypothesis over
$K \times D$ gives $|K|\Sigma_D \le |D|\Sigma_K + c|K||D|$. Then
$|K|(\Sigma_K + \Sigma_D) \le (|K| + |D|)\Sigma_K + c|K||D| = |s|\Sigma_K + c|K||D|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Retention $\ge$ work fraction]\label{cor:ret}
With $c = 0$ and $\sum_s r > 0$,
$\dfrac{\sum_{i\in K}r_i}{\sum_{i \in s} r_i} \ge \dfrac{|K|}{|s|}$; equivalently
$\Th(s) \le \Th(K)$. If some kept target strictly beats some skipped one, the inequality is
strict.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[An $\varepsilon$-accurate dial still wins]
If $|d_i - r_i| \le \varepsilon$ on $s$, then for every $\theta$,
\[
|K_\theta|\sum_{i \in s} r_i \le |s|\sum_{i \in K_\theta} r_i + 2\varepsilon |K_\theta||D_\theta|,
\qquad
\Th(s) \le \Th(K_\theta) + \frac{2\varepsilon|D_\theta|}{|s|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $i \in K_\theta$, $j \in D_\theta$ we have $d_j < \theta \le d_i$, so
$r_j \le d_j + \varepsilon < d_i + \varepsilon \le r_i + 2\varepsilon$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} with $c = 2\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rank error: discordance budgets}

\begin{definition}[Inversion set]
$\Disc(s;d,r) = \{(j,i)\in s\times s : d_j < d_i,\ r_i < r_j\}$, the pairs the dial ranks
backwards; $|\Disc|$ is the unnormalised Kendall discordance count.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Linear discordance budget]
If $0 \le r_i \le M$ on $s$, then for every $\theta$,
\[
|K_\theta|\sum_{i\in s} r_i \le |s|\sum_{i \in K_\theta} r_i + M\,|\Disc(s;d,r)|,
\qquad
\Th(s) \le \Th(K_\theta) + \frac{M|\Disc|}{|s||K_\theta|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Repeat Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} allowing an exceptional pair set. For
$(j,i) \in D_\theta\times K_\theta$ outside $\Disc$, the ordering $d_j < \theta \le d_i$
forces $r_j \le r_i$; each exceptional pair contributes at most $r_j - r_i \le M$.
\end{proof}

With $\Disc = \emptyset$ this collapses to Corollary~\ref{cor:ret}: the budget degrades
\emph{linearly}, not catastrophically, in the number of inversions.

\begin{definition}[Inversion mass]
$\IM(s;d,r) = \sum_{(j,i)\in\Disc(s;d,r)} (r_j - r_i) \ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened engine; no hypotheses on $r$]\label{thm:pp}
For any split $s = K \sqcup D$,
\[
|K|\sum_{i \in s} r_i \;\le\; |s|\sum_{i\in K} r_i \;+\; \sum_{(j,i)\in D\times K}(r_j - r_i)^{+},
\qquad x^{+} := \max(x,0).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The identity
$|K|\sum_{s} r - |s|\sum_{K} r = \sum_{(j,i)\in D\times K}(r_j - r_i)$ follows by expanding
the double sum as $|K|\Sigma_D - |D|\Sigma_K$; then $x \le x^{+}$ termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Refined budget]
For every $\theta$,
$|K_\theta|\sum_{i\in s} r_i \le |s|\sum_{i\in K_\theta} r_i + \IM(s;d,r)$, and
$\Th(s) \le \Th(K_\theta) + \IM/(|s||K_\theta|)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pp} it suffices that the kept mass
$\sum_{(j,i)\in D_\theta\times K_\theta}(r_j-r_i)^{+}$ is at most $\IM$: on such pairs
$d_j < \theta \le d_i$, so a nonzero positive part occurs exactly on discordant pairs, and
the kept mass is a subsum of $\IM$ over nonnegative terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Domination and consistency]
If $0 \le r \le M$ then $\IM \le M|\Disc|$, so the refined budget implies the linear one; if
every inversion has gap at most $g$ then $\IM \le g|\Disc|$; and $\IM = 0$ iff
$\Disc = \emptyset$.
\end{theorem}

On three targets with rates $(10,3,2)$ and dial $(10,2,3)$ there is exactly one inversion,
the refined penalty is $1$, and the crude penalty is $10$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact deficit decomposition]\label{thm:ledger}
Let
$\IM_\theta = \sum_{(j,i)\in D_\theta\times K_\theta}(r_j - r_i)^{+}$ and
$\CM_\theta = \sum_{(j,i)\in D_\theta\times K_\theta}(r_i - r_j)^{+}$. Then
\[
|K_\theta|\sum_{i \in s} r_i - |s|\sum_{i\in K_\theta} r_i \;=\; \IM_\theta - \CM_\theta,
\]
hence $|K_\theta|\sum_{s} r \le |s|\sum_{K_\theta} r + (\IM - \CM_\theta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply $x^{+} - (-x)^{+} = x$ termwise in the identity of Theorem~\ref{thm:pp}.
\end{proof}

The deficit is thus a ledger: the threshold pays the mass of inverted pairs and earns the
mass of correctly ordered ones. The one-sided bound is tight exactly when $\CM_\theta = 0$,
a regime no informative dial is in --- which is why a rank correlation far below $1$
suffices.

\section{The policy space collapses to one threshold}

\begin{definition}
$T \subseteq s$ is \emph{separated in $s$} if $r_j \le r_i$ for all $i \in T$,
$j \in s\setminus T$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]
For each $k \le |s|$ there is a $k$-subset of $s$ of maximal total rate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Every maximiser is separated]
If $T \subseteq s$ maximises total rate among subsets of its own cardinality, then $T$ is
separated in $s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Exchange: if $i \in T$, $j \notin T$, $r_j > r_i$, then $(T\setminus\{i\})\cup\{j\}$ has the
same size and strictly larger total.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minimum-work schedules may be taken separated]
If some $K \subseteq s$ satisfies $Q \le \sum_K r$, there is a separated $T \subseteq s$ with
$Q \le \sum_T r$ and $|T| \le |K'|$ for every quota-feasible $K' \subseteq s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}
For nonempty separated $T \subseteq s$ and $\theta = \min_{i\in T} r_i$ (dial $d = r$), we
have $T \subseteq K_\theta$; if $r \ge 0$ then $\sum_T r \le \sum_{K_\theta} r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold optimality]
Let $r \ge 0$ on $s$ and let $Q > 0$ be attainable by some subset. Then there is $\theta$
with $Q \le \sum_{i\in K_\theta} r_i$ and $\Th(s) \le \Th(K_\theta)$, strictly whenever the
threshold defers a target strictly worse than some retained one.
\end{theorem}

The deployment's search space therefore collapses from $2^{|s|}$ subsets to one real number.

\begin{definition}
$\mathrm{Tie}(\theta) = \{i \in s : r_i = \theta\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Excess equals tie class]
If $T$ is nonempty and separated in $s$ and $\theta = \min_T r$, then
$K_\theta \setminus T = \mathrm{Tie}(\theta)\setminus T$ as sets, hence
$|K_\theta| = |T| + |\mathrm{Tie}(\theta)\setminus T|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
If $r$ is injective on $s$, then $K_\theta = T$: the threshold policy reproduces the
minimum-work schedule exactly.
\end{corollary}

On three targets with rates $(3,3,1)$ and quota $3$ the minimal schedule has one element
while the threshold at $\theta = 3$ retains two, so the slack term is not vacuous.

\section{The arithmetic layer: the dial \emph{is} the rate}

Fix a target integer $N$; the sieve seeks primes $p$ dividing values $x^2 - N$.

\begin{definition}[Per-period rate; admissibility]
$\rho_N(p) = \frac{1}{p}\#\{x \in \{0,\dots,p-1\} : p \mid x^2 - N\}$. A prime is
\emph{admissible} for $N$ if it is odd, $N \not\equiv 0 \pmod p$, and $N$ is a quadratic
residue mod $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact solution counts]
Let $p$ be prime and $N \not\equiv 0 \pmod p$. If $p$ is odd and $N$ is a square mod $p$,
then $x^2 \equiv N$ has exactly two solutions; if $N$ is not a square mod $p$, exactly zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $N = b^2$, $b \ne 0$, then $x^2 = b^2$ iff $(x-b)(x+b) = 0$ iff $x = \pm b$ in the field
$\mathbb{Z}/p$, and $b \ne -b$ for odd $p$. Non-squares admit no solution by definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact rates]
An admissible odd prime has $\rho_N(p) = 2/p$; a prime for which $N$ is a non-residue has
$\rho_N(p) = 0$. Rates are nonnegative.
\end{corollary}

Thus the quadratic-residue dial is not a statistical proxy: up to the deterministic factor
$2/p$ it \emph{is} the rate.

\begin{theorem}[Null primes]
If $N$ is a quadratic non-residue mod $p$, then $p$ divides no value $x^2 - N$ over all
integers $x$; its empirical hit rate in every window is identically zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Null equaliser; deferral is the instrument]
Yield of any allocation is unchanged by deleting null targets, and moving any positive
budget off a null target onto a positive-rate target strictly increases yield. A hard tail
of null targets (in the measurement, $40$ of $400$) is unreachable by any amount of extra
sieving.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Small admissible primes carry the yield]
Among admissible odd primes, $p \le q$ implies $\rho_N(q) \le \rho_N(p)$, and
$\rho_N(p) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No ties on a factor base]
On a set $A$ of admissible odd primes, $p \mapsto 2/p$ is injective, so the threshold policy
is \emph{exactly} minimum-work: whenever a quota is attainable, some threshold's retained set
meets it, has minimum possible cardinality among quota-feasible schedules, and has
throughput at least that of sieving the whole base.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate rate]
If $A \subseteq \mathrm{FB}$ collects the admissible primes and every prime of
$\mathrm{FB}\setminus A$ is inadmissible, then
$\sum_{p\in\mathrm{FB}}\rho_N(p) = \sum_{p\in A} 2/p = 2H_A$ where $H_A = \sum_{p \in A} 1/p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Oracle target and exact headroom]
For nonempty admissible $A$, $\max_{p \in A}\rho_N(p) = 2/p_{\min}$ with $p_{\min} = \min A$,
and
\[
\frac{\max_{p\in A}\rho_N(p)}{\big(\sum_{p\in A}\rho_N(p)\big)/|A|} \;=\; \frac{|A|}{p_{\min}H_A},
\]
strictly less than the crude ceiling $|A|$ whenever $|A| > 1$.
\end{theorem}

For $N = 2$, the primes $7$ and $17$ are admissible, the aggregate rate of $\{7,17\}$ is
exactly $2/7 + 2/17$, and the oracle target is $7$ with rate $2/7$.

\section{The honest trade-off}

\begin{theorem}[Throughput is monotone in $\theta$]
For a concordant dial and $\theta_1 \le \theta_2$ with $K_{\theta_2} \ne \emptyset$,
$\Th(K_{\theta_1}) \le \Th(K_{\theta_2})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total yield is antitone in $\theta$]
For nonnegative rates and $\theta_1 \le \theta_2$,
$\sum_{K_{\theta_2}} r \le \sum_{K_{\theta_1}} r$, strictly as soon as the higher threshold
defers a target of positive rate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Quota-admissible thresholds form a down-set: if $\theta_2$ meets the quota and
$\theta_1 \le \theta_2$, so does $\theta_1$. The operating point is the largest
quota-feasible threshold.
\end{corollary}

So ``$+28.9\%$ throughput at $89.5\%$ retention'' names the two sides of a genuine
trade-off: the flip is correct exactly when work, not relations, is the binding constraint.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Allocation audit, $O(n)$.} From $r$ compute $\AM(r)$, $\HM(r)$, $r_{\max}$:
these give exactly the outcomes of the baseline ($B\AM$), the inverse-rate policy ($B\HM$),
and the concentrator/oracle ($Br_{\max}$) \emph{before} spending anything.

\paragraph{(B) Clip-line evaluation, $O(n)$.} $Y(f) = Bn/T + f(\Sigma - n^2/T)$ on
$f \in [0, B/n]$; a positive slope (equivalently, unequal rates) puts the optimum of the
family at $f = B/n$, i.e. at the uniform baseline.

\paragraph{(C) Optimal deferral under a quota, $O(n\log n)$.} Sort targets by dial value
descending, accumulate until the quota is met; the prefix is a minimum-work quota-feasible
schedule, realised by the threshold equal to its smallest dial value.

\paragraph{(D) Discordance ledger, $O(n^2)$.} Enumerate ordered pairs to accumulate
$\Disc$, $\IM$, and at a given $\theta$ the paid and earned masses $\IM_\theta, \CM_\theta$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:ledger} then predicts the retention deficit exactly.

\section{Numerical illustration}

Rates $(1,2,5)$ with budget $3$: $\AM = 8/3 \approx 2.667$ and
$\HM = 3/1.7 \approx 1.765$, so the inverse-rate yield $\approx 5.29$ against the baseline's
$8$ --- a loss of about $34\%$, of the same sign as $-17.6\%$. The concentrator yields $15$,
which is also the oracle bound; the headroom ratio $15/8 = 1.875$ sits below the crude
ceiling $3$. Deferring the worst target retains $7/8 = 87.5\%$ of relations for
$2/3 \approx 66.7\%$ of the work (measured: $89.5\%$ for $71.7\%$), raising throughput from
$8/3$ to $7/2$.

Ledger example: rates $(10,3,2)$, dial $(10,2,3)$; one inversion, $\IM = 1$, crude penalty
$10$.

Arithmetic example: $N = 2$, base $\{7,17\}$; both admissible ($3^2\equiv 2 \bmod 7$,
$6^2 \equiv 2 \bmod 17$), aggregate rate $2/7 + 2/17 = 48/119$, oracle target $7$, headroom
ratio $2/\big(7(1/7 + 1/17)\big) = 17/12 \approx 1.417 < 2$. By contrast $2$ is a non-residue
mod $5$, so $5$ has rate exactly $0$.

\section{Discussion}

A predictor and a policy are different objects. The results comparing inverse-rate
allocation with the baseline never mention a predictor, so any experiment measuring that
comparison is measuring $1 - \HM(r)/\AM(r)$ and calling it dial quality. Inverse-rate
allocation is the unique \emph{equalising} policy, and equity across targets is exactly
orthogonal to total throughput; if per-target equity is genuinely required, the AM--HM gap
is the exact price.

Engineering clips are often invisible to analysis because they look like special cases; here
the clip is a coordinate on a segment joining two named policies and its contribution is
monotone. For deferral, absolute error is the wrong currency --- inversions are
dimensionless, the inversion mass has the right units, and the deficit is an exact ledger of
paid and earned mass, which is why a modest rank correlation suffices.

Finally, in this application the rate is a closed-form arithmetic quantity, $2/p$ or $0$: so
ties are impossible and threshold policies are exactly optimal, and the hard tail is
provably unreachable. Any scheme that spends \emph{more} on low-yield targets pours budget
into an exactly null set; that is why the unclipped run lost more than $100\%$ of baseline.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The model is linear in sieve length: startup cost, saturation, and
downstream linear-algebra dependencies are absent, and total yield is a proxy for a
full-rank relation matrix. The throughput objective assumes work proportional to the number
of retained targets at a common sieve depth; mixed policies (threshold \emph{and} variable
depth) are not covered.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Nonlinear yield curves.} Replace $r_i\ell_i$ by a concave $g_i(\ell_i)$
modelling saturation; does the sign of the inverse-rate rule survive, and does the clip
remain a monotone coordinate?
\item \textbf{Analytic control of the headroom.} The headroom ratio is exactly
$|A|/(p_{\min}H_A)$; combining this with classical estimates for sums of prime reciprocals
over an admissible (density $1/2$) subfamily should yield a closed-form asymptotic for the
maximal adaptive headroom of a factor base of bound $B$.
\item \textbf{Pareto frontier in $\theta$.} Throughput and total yield move oppositely;
characterise the optimal $\theta$ for a stated exchange rate between wall-clock and
relations.
\item \textbf{Ledger-driven dial design.} A dial's value for deferral is
$\CM_\theta - \IM_\theta$, not its correlation; design dials that maximise net mass directly.
\item \textbf{Beyond a single threshold.} Allowing depth to vary on the retained set
reintroduces the allocation problem inside it, where the linear model says ``concentrate'';
reconciling this with practice requires the nonlinear model of direction~1.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The four measured numbers --- $-17.6\%$, $-146.7\%$, $+8.6\%$, $+74.8\%$ --- are one theorem
read four ways: the harmonic mean lies below the arithmetic mean; the clipped family is the
affine segment joining them; the maximum lies above the mean; and the maximum is exactly the
ceiling. None measures the dial. What the dial is good for is deferral, where separation
makes retention dominate the work fraction, imperfection costs at most a linear inversion
mass, and the optimum over all $2^n$ schedules is a single threshold. On a quadratic-sieve
factor base, where the rate is exactly $2/p$ or exactly $0$, that threshold policy is
exactly minimum-work, and the barren targets are barren by elementary number theory rather
than bad luck.

